2026-04-03 00:25:38 - Agent_20260403002229 - INFO - [+] Agent signaled completion: Reviewed 6 CodeQL queries (20260403000452, 20260403000534, 20260403000746, 20260403000913, 20260403001142, 20260403001419) targeting the google-json JsonSanitizer library. 

Key findings:
1. TRUE POSITIVE (High): JsonSanitizer.sanitize() does not escape standalone HTML special characters (<, >, and HTML/script tags) in JSON string values. Only </script and ]]> patterns are escaped. Opening <script> tags, <img onerror=...>, <svg onload=...> and other HTML tags pass through unescaped. This is an XSS bypass when the sanitizer output is embedded in HTML body context instead of inside <script> tags. Confirmed by test at JsonSanitizerTest.java:59 which shows "<script>foo()</script>" → "<script>foo()<\/script>" (opening tag unescaped) and line 68 showing "<b>Hello</b>" passes through completely unchanged.

2. TRUE POSITIVE (High): EvalMinifier.minify() inherits the same XSS bypass since it calls JsonSanitizer internally and the unescaped HTML characters in JSON strings persist in its output.

3. FALSE POSITIVE: Unicode escape sequences (\u2028, \u2029, \u005d, etc.) flagged by Query 2 and 5 are legitimate sanitization controls properly encoding dangerous characters.

4. FALSE POSITIVE: StringBuilder operations and character literals '<', '>', '-' flagged by Queries 3, 4, and 6 are internal comparison/detection logic and buffer operations, not unescaped output sinks.
